+++</md></sn><sn><md>#### QN :  Describe amino acid classification with one example in each group


**On the basis of the composition of the side chain or R group**

| Category | Description / Key Feature | Examples |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **1) Simple amino acids** | No functional group in the side chain | Glycine, alanine |
| **2) Hydroxy amino acids** | Contain a hydroxy group in their side chain | Serine, Threonine |
| **3) S containing amino acids** | Possess a sulfur atom in the side chain | Methionine, Cystine |
| **4) Acidic amino acids** | Have a carboxyl group in the side chain | Aspartic acid, glutamic acid |
| **5) Amino acid amides** | Derivatives of acidic amino acids in which one of the carboxyl groups has been transformed into an amide group | Asparagine, Glutamine |
| **6) Basic amino acids** | Possess an amino group in the side chain | Lysine, arginine |
| **7) Heterocyclic amino acids** | Side chain is a ring which possesses at least one atom other than carbon | Tryptophan, Histidine |
| **8) Aromatic amino acid** | Have a benzene ring in the side chain | Phenylalanine, Tyrosine |

---

**On the basis of number of amino and carboxylic group**

| Category | Description | Examples |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **1) Mono amino-mono carboxylic amino acid** | Contains one amino group and one carboxylic group | Glycine, alanine |
| **2) Mono amino dicarboxylic amino acid** | Contains one amino group and two carboxylic groups | Aspartic acid, glutamic acid |
| **3) Diamino mono carboxylic amino acid** | Contains two amino groups and one carboxylic group | Lysine, arginine |

---

**On the basis of the polarity of the side chain or R group**

| Category | Description / Key Feature | Examples |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **1) Amino acid with non-polar R group** | Includes five amino acids with aliphatic R groups, two with aromatic rings, and one S-containing amino acid. The R group is hydrocarbon in nature, hence hydrophobic. | Alanine, valine, leucine, isoleucine, Proline, Phenylalanine, Tryptophan, Methionine |
| **2) Amino acids with polar but uncharged R group** | R groups are more soluble in water (hydrophilic). Polarity may be due to a hydroxyl group, a sulfide group, or an amide group. Includes 7 amino acids. | Glycine, serine, Threonine, tyrosine, cysteine, asparagines, glutamine |
| **3) Amino acids with positively charged R groups** | Diamino mono carboxylic acids; their side chain contains an extra amino group which imparts basic properties to them. | Lysine, arginine, Histidine |

